  {% set yesimPrimary = providerB.codes | findCode(providerB.primaryCode) %}

  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>Holafly</strong> if you want one flat price and never want to think about a data meter again. Pick <strong>Yesim</strong> if you're a repeat traveler who'd rather build up a rollover data balance and keep a real refund option open. They're not really pricing the same thing: Holafly sells unlimited access by the day, Yesim sells a dollar balance you draw down by the gigabyte.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: Unlimited Flat Rate or a Pay & Fly Balance</h2>
  <p>
    Holafly's whole product is unlimited data at one flat price per day or region, no gigabyte counting required. In the US, most of Europe, and Asia that's <strong>$1.99/day</strong>; a global plan covering 200+ destinations runs <strong>$9.90/day</strong>. There's a fair-use speed reduction after very heavy same-day use, though Holafly doesn't publish the exact threshold the way some competitors do.
  </p>
  <p>
    Yesim takes the opposite approach with its "Pay & Fly" plans: you buy a dollar balance, priced from $1.10/GB, and whatever you don't use rolls over to your next trip instead of expiring when the current one ends. Yesim also advertises automatic switching across a claimed 800+ partner networks ("SwitchLess Networks") so the eSIM hops to whichever local carrier has the best signal; that's Yesim's own claim, not something we've independently measured. Either way, Yesim is data-only, same as Holafly: no bundled phone number on either side, so voice calls on both run over apps like WhatsApp using the data connection.
  </p>

