      <li><strong>Yesim:</strong> {{ providerB.primaryCode }}, {{ yesimPrimary.discount | lower }}. <a href="/yesim/">Full code list &rarr;</a></li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:.75rem;margin-bottom:0;color:#63553F;">Holafly's code is ours, so we show it first; it covers both single-eSIM and monthly-plan purchases. Yesim's code is a public first-purchase-only code, not one of ours yet. Returning Yesim customers should try <strong>WELCOMETOYESIM</strong> (15% off) or <strong>TRAVELFREE</strong> (a flat &euro;3 off) instead, both covered on the <a href="/yesim/">Yesim page</a>.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Is Holafly or Yesim the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with Holafly if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You genuinely don't know how much data you'll use and don't want to track it</li>
        <li>Heavy streaming, video calls, or hotspot use on a single trip</li>
        <li>China is on the itinerary and you want the built-in VPN</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with Yesim if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You travel often and want a balance that carries over instead of expiring per trip</li>
        <li>You might not use the whole plan and want a real refund option, not just a failed-activation refund</li>
        <li>You'd rather buy data in dollars than track a flat daily rate</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Holafly's China eSIM has a VPN built in.</strong> Google Maps, WhatsApp, and Gmail just work without installing a separate VPN app first. Yesim has no equivalent, so a China trip on Yesim means sorting out your own VPN regardless of plan.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Yesim's refund guarantee is genuinely unusual for this category.</strong> Most eSIM providers on this site only refund a plan that never activated at all. Yesim advertises a 30-day money-back guarantee on plans you simply didn't end up using, which is the kind of flexibility that can offset its higher per-day price if you tend to buy ahead of a trip and aren't sure you'll need the full plan. If a cheaper flat-rate alternative to Holafly is more your speed, <a href="/airhub/">Airhub</a>'s per-GB pricing is worth a look too.
  </p>
  <p>
    For device setup and eSIM basics that apply to both providers, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>Holafly vs Yesim FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, Holafly or Yesim</h3>
      <p>Holafly, in every destination we checked. Holafly sits in our "Cheap" price bracket; Yesim sits in "Expensive." Yesim's rollover balance and refund guarantee are the trade-off for the higher price, not a cheaper rate.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does either one roll over unused data to a future trip</h3>
      <p>Yesim does, through its Pay &amp; Fly balance: you buy a dollar amount and whatever's left over carries to your next trip. Holafly sells unlimited access per day or region instead, so there's no unused balance to roll over in the first place.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which one is better for China</h3>
      <p>Holafly, if you don't want to set up a separate VPN: its China eSIM includes one built in. Yesim has no equivalent, so you'd need your own VPN solution regardless of plan.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Can I get a refund if I don't use the plan</h3>
      <p>Yesim's 30-day money-back guarantee covers plans that were never used, not just ones that failed to activate. We haven't found an equivalent policy advertised for Holafly beyond the standard failed-activation case.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

